From aef9c535f39c0cf1f9d6b63b8c3b803f52268d91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NickLabs Orbis <orbis@nicklabs.invalid>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 20:50:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] drm/amdgpu: PS4 GPU buffer shrinker and active GTT trim
 (Orbis ge-0014)

NickLabs Orbis unified-memory work (U25/U26). The PS4 CPU and GPU share one GDDR5 pool,
but amdgpu never returns idle GTT/system buffers to Linux under memory pressure: only
TTM's pages_limit swapout at allocation time exists. This adds, for Liverpool/Gladius
and only with amdgpu.orbis_shrinker=1:

- backup-capable user buffers: TTM_TT_FLAG_EXTERNAL | EXTERNAL_MAPPABLE plus a shmem
  backup (ttm_tt_setup_backup), set after ttm_sg_tt_init() so the page array is kept;
  bind/populate/unpopulate tell these apart from imported dma-bufs; userptr buffers drop
  the capability;
- populate restores backed-up content (ttm_tt_restore) or starts fresh for purged
  discardable buffers;
- a memory shrinker (pattern of xe_shrinker.c, same 6.15 TTM helpers) that walks the
  SYSTEM and TT LRUs with trylocks and calls ttm_bo_shrink(): regular TT->SYSTEM move
  (move_notify, GART unbind), then backup to shmem or purge. Exported/imported
  dma-bufs, kernel buffers, ghost objects and pinned buffers are skipped; direct
  reclaim does not wait for the GPU. Backed-up pages are ordinary shmem pages that the
  core VM swaps (zram on Orbis);
- active trim: writing a lower orbis_gtt_budget evicts GTT buffers in LRU order until
  usage fits, instead of waiting for the next allocation;
- sysfs orbis_gtt_reclaim: counters (shrinkable, purgeable, backed up, shrunk, purged,
  restored, trim evictions) and "scan <pages> [writeback]" for verification.

Status: written against the pinned 6.15.4 interfaces; not compiled or tested.
